QB.add(38,[{"n":1,"q":"Match the haematological malignancies with their predominant age group. 1. Acute Myeloid Leukemia a. Children 2. Acute Lymphoblastic Leukaemia b. Adolescent to middle-aged 3. Chronic Lymphocytic Leukaemia c. Middle age to elderly 4. Chronic Myeloid Leukaemia d. Elderly\n1. Acute Myeloid Leukemia a. Children\n2. Acute Lymphoblastic Leukaemia b. Adolescent to middle-aged\n3. Chronic Lymphocytic Leukaemia c. Middle age to elderly\n4. Chronic Myeloid Leukaemia d. Elderly","o":[{"t":"1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c"},{"t":"1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d"},{"t":"1-b, 2-a, 3-c, 4-d"},{"t":"1-a, 2-b, 3-d, 4-c"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hematological malignancy Predominant age group"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) Adolescent to middle-aged Hematological malignancy Predominant age group"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) Children"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) Elderly"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) Middle age to elderly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is the second most common leukaemia in adults and the most common type of acute leukaemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) accounts for approximately one-third of all childhood malignancies and is the most common form of cancer in children ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is the most common leukaemia in Western countries and is most commonly seen in the geriatric population ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) can occur in all age groups from adults to the elderly population, and the incidence increases with age but is rare in the pediatric population."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 75-year-old man presents with complaints of fatigue, weight loss, and generalized lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. The patient gives a history of working in a factory with exposure to ionizing radiation 20 years ago. Laboratory tests reveal lymphocytosis, anemia, and thrombocytopenia. Which of the following haematological malignancies is least likely to be associated with his prior radiation exposure?","o":[{"t":"Acute Myeloid Leukemia"},{"t":"Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia"},{"t":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia"},{"t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is the only leukemia not associated with radiation exposure and is the most commonly seen in the elderly ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a monoclonal proliferation of mature B lymphocytes defined by an absolute number of malignant cells (absolute lymphocyte count) in the blood (5 × 10 9 /mL) for consecutive 3 months ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The majority of the cases are asymptomatic and are mostly incidental diagnoses."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Family history and genetic predisposition may contribute to CLL but not radiation exposure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of malignant B cells under this count in the blood without nodal, spleen, or liver involvement and absent cytopenias is a precursor of monoclonal B cell lymphocytosis (MBL)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Note: Exposure to radiation is one of the risk factors for Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML), Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) and Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"AML (Option A) is an aggressive tumor comprised of immature myeloid lineage blasts , associated with diverse acquired mutations, which interfere with myeloid differentiation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"ALL (Option B) is characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphoblasts in the bone marrow. Peripheral blood smear examination typically shows a predominance of lymphoblasts, which are larger in size compared to mature lymphocytes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CML (Option D) is a myeloproliferative neoplasm characterized by the excessive production of granulocytes in the bone marrow. Peripheral blood smear examination in CML typically shows increased numbers of granulocytes, including mature neutrophils, myelocytes, and metamyelocytes, as well as basophilia."}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 68 year old male presents with fatigue and unintentional weight loss. Physical examination reveals cervical lymphadenopathy and mild splenomegaly. Blood investigations show a white blood cell count of 75,000/µL with marked lymphocytosis. A peripheral blood smear reveals the following picture. Which of the following genetic alterations is frequently observed in this condition?","o":[{"t":"Deletion of chromosome 13q14"},{"t":"A translocation involving chromosomes 9 and 22"},{"t":"A translocation involving chromosomes 8 and 14"},{"t":"Inversion of chromosome 16"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation and peripheral smear findings of smudge cells are characteristic of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL). The most common genetic alteration associated with CLL is the deletion of chromosome 13q14 and is often associated with a favourable prognosis ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cytogenetic abnormalities Gene mutati Overexpression"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ons"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Deletion of chromosome 13q14 SF3B1 ZAP - 70 Trisomy 12 NOTCH1 Intracellular Tyrosine kinase. Deletion of 11q (associated with MYD88 Present usually inside the T cell. aggressive disease in young ATM Aberrant expression in CLL (B cells). patients) TP53 ZAP-70 positive fi Early treatment Deletion of 17p is recommended."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"BCL 2 It inhibits apoptosis"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Translocation involving chromosomes 9 and 22 (Option B) refers to the Philadelphia chromosome, characteristic of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Translocation involving chromosomes 8 and 14 (Option C) is characteristic of Burkitt's lymphoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Inversion of chromosome 16 (Option D) is seen in Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML), particularly in the subtype, Acute Myelomonocytic Leukemia with eosinophilia (M4-eos)."}],"qi":[6606]},{"n":4,"q":"Which of the following microRNAs is most commonly associated with the pathophysiology of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)?","o":[{"t":"miR-34a"},{"t":"miR-21"},{"t":"miR-146a"},{"t":"miR15A/miR16A"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"miR15A and miR16A are frequently deleted or downregulated in CLL. These microRNAs regulate the expression of anti-apoptotic proteins. Loss of miR-15a/miR-16-1 leads to their overexpression contributing to the pathogenesis of CLL by promoting cell survival."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pathophysiology of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most common cytogenetic abnormality in CLL, del(13)(q14.3) , leads to the loss of the miR15/16 cluster, which plays a key role in CLL development."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Normally, miR15A/miR16A suppresses the expression of anti-apoptotic genes like BCL2, CCND1, CCND3, and CDK6 ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The deletion of this cluster results in the overexpression of these genes, promoting increased cell survival ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The loss of other microRNAs, such as miR-181a , leads to the overexpression of proteins like MCL-1 and TCL1, which further enhance cell survival."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Moreover, the overexpression of miR-155 , an onco-miR linked to B-cell transformation, has been observed in the majority of CLL patients."}]},{"n":5,"q":"A 70 year old female presents with complaints of fatigue and recurrent infections over the past several months. On examination, there is generalized lymphadenopathy and mild splenomegaly. Blood tests revealed lymphocytosis and flow cytometry of peripheral blood showed the following findings. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Mantle Cell Lymphoma"},{"t":"Marginal Zone Lymphoma"},{"t":"Hodgkin’s Lymphoma"},{"t":"Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation, including recurrent infections, with flow cytometry findings showing CD5 positive, CD19 positive and CD23 positive while CD20 is dimly expressed, points towards the diagnosis of Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (which is a B-cell tumor but it expresses CD5)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Flow cytometry is the most appropriate investigation to confirm the diagnosis and characterize the immunophenotype of the abnormal lymphocytes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It allows for identifying clonal B-cells expressing characteristic surface markers such as CD5, CD19, CD20 (Dim), and CD23 ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It helps confirm the diagnosis of CLL and determine the extent of the disease."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"CD markers:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"T-lymphocytes: CD1 to 8, CD28"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"B-lymphocytes: CD10, CD19 to 23, CD40"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Disease CD5 CD10 CD19 CD20 CD23 Cyclin D1 Surface Ig"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CLL + - + + (dim) + - + (dim)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Mantle cell + - + + - + + lymphoma (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Marginal - - + + - - + zone lymph oma (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Follicular - + + + + - lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hodgkin’s Lymphoma (Option C) shows CD15 and CD30 positivity."}],"qi":[6609,6612]},{"n":6,"q":"Which staging system primarily assesses Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) based on the extent of lymphocytosis and the presence of lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, and hepatomegaly?","o":[{"t":"Binet Staging System"},{"t":"Rai Staging System"},{"t":"International Staging System"},{"t":"WHO Staging System"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The Rai Staging System is a widely used method to stage CLL. It categorizes patients into three risk groups based on the extent of disease involvement :"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Risk Stage Involves"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Low 0 Only Lymphocytosis"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Intermediate I/II Lymphocytosis with enlarged lymph"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"nodes, with or without splenomegaly or hepatomegaly."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"High risk III/IV Lymphocytosis along with anaemia"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"or thrombocytopenia due to bone marrow involvement."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Binet Staging System (Option A) categorizes CLL based on the number of areas of lymphadenopathy and the presence of anaemia or thrombocytopenia ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Stage Involves"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"A <3 areas of lymphadenopathy Stage Involves"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"B ‡3 areas of lymphadenopathy"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"C Hemoglobin £10 g/dL and/or platelets <100,000/mL"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The International Staging System and WHO Staging System (Options C & D) are not specific to CLL but are used for staging other types of cancers, such as multiple myeloma and solid tumors."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A 67-year-old male with a history of chronic lymphocytic leukemia presents with sudden worsening of night sweats and unintentional weight loss. Laboratory investigations reveal elevated lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) levels and thrombocytopenia associated with a marked increase in lymphadenopathy. What is the most appropriate next step in management for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Initiate treatment with rituximab, cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, and prednisone (R-CHOP) regimen"},{"t":"Obtain an FDG-PET scan to guide further diagnostic workup."},{"t":"Begin treatment with fludarabine, cyclophosphamide, and rituximab (FCR) regimen."},{"t":"Start targeted therapy with ibrutinib for CLL"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient presents with clinical deterioration and features suggestive of Richter transformation , which warrants further diagnostic evaluation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FDG-PET scan can help localize areas of increased metabolic activity, guiding the selection of lymph nodes for biopsy to confirm the diagnosis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Richter transformation (RT):"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Definition Richter’s transformation refers to the transformation of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"CLL into an aggressive lymphoma, most commonly Diffuse Large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), and can rarely include Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL)."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Risk Factor Bulky lymphadenopathy s NOTCH1 mutations"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Del(17)(p13.1)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Clinical Rapid progression of adenopathy, often localized. Signs Constitutional symptoms: fatigue, night sweats, fever,"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"weight loss. Elevated LDH levels are common."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Diagnosis 18FDG-PET/CT scan is the first step to localize areas"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"for biopsy. Excisional biopsy is preferred; fine-needle aspiration is discouraged."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Treatment Treatment decisions should be based on confirmed"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"diagnosis and staging. DLBCL Richter’s transformation is usually treated with combination chemoimmunotherapy (e.g., R-CHOP or dose-adjusted EPOCH-R )."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"FCR regimen (Option C) is used for CLL/SLL treatment, not RT."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ibrutinib (Option D) is a targeted therapy for CLL/SLL and is not the preferred treatment for RT."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 48-year-old female presents to the clinic with a history of easy bruising, petechiae, and recurrent episodes of mucosal bleeding. She reports feeling fatigued and weak for the past few weeks. Laboratory investigations reveal severe thrombocytopenia and hemolytic anaemia. Peripheral smear shows multiple spherocytes but no schistocytes. The direct antiglobulin test is positive. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Evans syndrome"},{"t":"Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura"},{"t":"HIV-related cytopenias"},{"t":"Occult malignancy"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient in the scenario presents with Coombs-positive hemolytic anaemia (AIHA) and severe thrombocytopenia, consistent with Evans syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Evans syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Evans syndrome is characterized by the simultaneous or sequential presence of autoimmune hemolytic anaemia (AIHA) and immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If they are not responding to steroids (no rise in platelet count), chemotherapy is recommended based on the age of the patient and comorbidities."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Explanation:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Evans syndrome Thrombotic HIV-related Occult malignancy (Option A) thrombocytopenic cytopenias (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"purpura (TTP) (Option C ruled out) (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pat Autoimmune Formation of Various mechanisms Occult malignancy ho- destruction of platelet-rich thrombi include bone is a less common phy RBCs and platele in small blood marrow suppression , cause of hemolytic siol ts. vessels. autoimmune anaemia and ogy destruction, or thrombocytopenia."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"infections ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Pre Hemolytic Microangiopathic Anaemia and/or Hemolytic anaemia sen anaemia and hemolytic anaemia, thrombocytopenia and thrombocytopen tati thrombocytopenia. thrombocytopenia, secondary to HIV ia are less common on end-organ damage. infection. presentations."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Dia Coombs-positive Low platelet count, f HIV serology. Occult malignancy gno hemolytic ragmented RBCs may have additional stic anaemia , low (schistocytes). features suggestive fea platelet count. of cancer, such as ture weight loss and s lymphadenopathy."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Evans syndrome Thrombotic HIV-related Occult malignancy (Option A) thrombocytopenic cytopenias (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"purpura (TTP) (Option C ruled out) (Option B ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tre Glucocorticoids, Plasma exchange, Management Treatment is atm rituximab , and corticosteroids, directed at HIV directed at underlyin ent mycophenolate rituximab. infection with g malignancy,"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"mofetil are used. antiretroviral chemotherapy, and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"therapy (ART) and radiation therapy. supportive care."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 68-year-old male presents with fatigue, night sweats, and unintentional weight loss. On examination, generalized lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly are noted. Blood tests reveal a white blood cell count of 90,000/µL. Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is suspected. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely to support the diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Lymphocytosis, thrombocytopenia, and bone marrow showing diffuse infiltration by small mature lymphocytes with expression of CD5 and CD19"},{"t":"Pancytopenia, blast cells on peripheral smear, and lymph node biopsy showing Reed-Sternberg cells"},{"t":"Thrombocytosis, neutrophilia, and bone marrow showing hypercellularity with increased myeloid precursors"},{"t":"Eosinophilia, basophilia, and bone marrow showing increased plasma cells with M protein spike"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complete blood count Total leukocyte count is elevated"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Decreased levels of hemoglobin Thrombocytopenia"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Peripheral smear Small lymphocytes with condensed chromatin and scant smear CLL \" data-author=\"Prof. Erhabor Osaro\" cytoplasm . data-hash=\"9706\" data-license=\"CC BY SA 4.0\" These mature-looking lymphocytes are highly fragile data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C and can get disrupted in the process of making smears, hronic_Lymphocytic_Leukaemia_%28lymphocytosis_an known as smudge cells . d_smear_cells%29.jpg\" data-tags=\"April2025\" Autoimmune hemolytic anemia: Presence of"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"TBhPPjMOK1744402364.png\" /> Normal RBC: Central pallor {{caption_text}} Spherocyte RBC: Central pallor is absent (small)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Lymph node biopsy Diffuse effacement of nodal architecture"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Flow cytometry (IOC) CD 19 positive"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"CD 20 (dim) positive CD 23 positive CD 5 positive (It is a B cell tumor expressing the T cell marker)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pancytopenia, blast cells on peripheral smear, and lymph node biopsy showing Reed-Sternberg cells (Option B) describes Hodgkin Lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thrombocytosis, neutrophilia, and bone marrow showing hypercellularity with increased myeloid precursors (Option C) suggests a myeloproliferative disorder, such as chronic myeloid leukemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Eosinophilia, basophilia, and bone marrow showing increased plasma cells with M protein spike (Option D) findings are consistent with multiple myeloma."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 68-year-old man presents with fatigue, enlarged lymph nodes, and recurrent infections. Peripheral blood smear reveals lymphocytosis with smudge cells. Flow cytometry confirms the diagnosis of chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL). As you assess his prognosis, which of the following factors would you consider as good prognostic indicators for CLL?","o":[{"t":"Deletion of 13q14 chromosome"},{"t":"Unmutated Ig heavy chain"},{"t":"ZAP-70"},{"t":"Deletion of 17p chromosome"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The presence of sole deletion of the 13q14 chromosome is associated with more indolent disease, prolonged survival, and good response to traditional therapies."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prognostic Factors Good Prognosis Bad Prognosis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cytogenetic Abnormalities Deletion of 13q14 Deletion of 17p (poorest prognosis)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"(Option D ruled out) Deletion of 11q NOTCH1 mutations"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Immunoglobulin Heavy Hypermutated IGHV Unmutated IGHV (Option B ruled Chain out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"LDH Levels Normal Elevated"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Other Biomarkers Low b2-microglobulin High b2-microglobulin levels"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"levels"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"CD38 expression - +"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"ZAP-70 (Option C ruled out) - +"}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 75-year-old male presents with fatigue, unintentional weight loss, and enlarged lymph nodes. The patient is a known case of T2 DM under control with medications. Laboratory tests reveal lymphocytosis with a high percentage of mature lymphocytes. Flow cytometry confirms the presence of CD5, CD19, CD20, and CD23 positive B cells. What is the most appropriate initial treatment for this patient?","o":[{"t":"Fludarabine + Cyclophosphamide + Rituximab"},{"t":"Rituximab monotherapy"},{"t":"Ibrutinib + venetoclax + idelalisib"},{"t":"Chlorambucil + Obinutuzumab"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Based on the clinical presentation and flow cytometry findings, the patient is likely to have chronic lymphocytic leukaemia (CLL). Given the patient's age of 75, the most appropriate initial treatment option would be Chlorambucil + Obinutuzumab ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It has shown efficacy in improving overall survival and progression-free survival in patients above 65 years of age with comorbidities ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Another regime is Bendamustine + Rituximab which is also effective in these patients."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptoms Indicating the need for Therapy in CLL:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Evidence of progressive marrow failure (worsening of anaemia or thrombocytopenia, not due to autoimmune destruction)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Massive (‡6 cm below costal margin), progressive, or symptomatic splenomegaly"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Massive (‡10 cm), progressive, or symptomatic lymphadenopathy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Progressive lymphocytosis with an increase of ‡50% over 2 months or"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"lymphocyte doubling time < 6 months"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Autoimmune anaemia or thrombocytopenia not responsive to standard therapy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptomatic or functional extranodal involvement"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Constitutional symptoms (one or more of the following: unintentional weight loss ‡10% over 6 months, significant fatigue, fevers ‡100.5°C for 2+ weeks without infection, night sweats for >1 month without infection)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Treatment protocol:"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"<65 yea No comorbidities rs Chemotherapeutic drugs include FCR (Option A ruled"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"out) . In older editions, it includes PCR (Pentostatin, Cyclophosphamide, and Rituximab) F: Fludarabine (DOC for Chronic lymphocytic leukemia) C: Cyclophosphamide R: Rituximab"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":">65 yea Comorbidities are present (Hypertension or DM, but rs HbA1C is well controlled)"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Chemotherapeutic drugs Chlorambucil Obinutuzumab Alternative drugs Bendamustine with Rituximab Antiapoptotic drug Venetoclax B cell drugs that act directly on B cell tumors B cell signaling inhibitors specifically affect the cells of Chronic lymphocytic leukemia but don't cause damage to the adjacent cells present in the bone marrow Drugs include Ibrutinib (Bruton tyrosine kinase inhibitor) Idelalisib"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rituximab monotherapy (Option B) is not typically recommended as the first-line treatment for CLL due to its limited efficacy compared to combination therapies."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Ibrutinib + Venetoclax + Idelalisib (Option C): Ibrutinib is a BTK inhibitor , Venetoclax is a BCL-2 inhibitor , and Idelalisib is a PI3K inhibitor. These agents target specific pathways involved in CLL cell survival and proliferation. While this combination may offer advantages in terms of efficacy and tolerability, it is usually reserved for patients with relapsed or refractory CLL."}]},{"n":12,"q":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of alemtuzumab used in treating Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia?","o":[{"t":"Targets the CD20 antigen on B cells, leading to cell lysis."},{"t":"Inhibits BCL-2, preventing apoptosis of CLL cells."},{"t":"Binds to the CD52 antigen on lymphocytes, leading to complement-mediated cell lysis and antibody-dependent cytotoxicity."},{"t":"Blocks the BTK signaling pathway, inhibiting B cell proliferation."}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alemtuzumab is a recombinant human IgG1 kappa monoclonal antibody."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It binds to the CD52 cell surface marker on B and T cells , monocytes, macrophages, and other bone marrow cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alemtuzumab therapy depletes lymphocytes, suppressing B cells for 6 to 12 months and T cells for 12 to 24 months."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Side effects include serious infusion reactions and bone marrow suppression. It is a potent immunosuppressive agent that predisposes patients to opportunistic bacterial, fungal, and viral infections."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Targets the CD20 antigen on B cells, leading to cell lysis (Option A): This describes rituximab , not alemtuzumab. Rituximab is a monoclonal antibody used in various B-cell malignancies."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Inhibits BCL-2, preventing apoptosis of CLL cells (Option B): This refers to venetoclax , a BCL-2 inhibitor used in CLL to promote apoptosis of malignant cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Blocks the BTK signaling pathway, inhibiting B cell proliferation (Option D): This describes ibrutinib , a Bruton's tyrosine kinase (BTK) inhibitor used in CLL and other B-cell disorders."}]},{"n":13,"q":"A 75-year-old man with chronic lymphocytic leukemia presents with progressive fatigue, night sweats, and weight loss. Despite initiating appropriate treatment, the patient develops fever and respiratory distress. A CT scan of the chest shows bilateral infiltrates. His condition deteriorates rapidly, and he requires intensive care. Which of the following mechanisms most likely contributes to the leading cause of death in patients with CLL?","o":[{"t":"T-cell dysfunction causing viral infections"},{"t":"Hypogammaglobulinemia increasing bacterial infections"},{"t":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia causing organ failure"},{"t":"Richter transformation to aggressive lymphoma"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypogammaglobulinemia is commonly seen in CLL patients and predisposes them to bacterial infections, which is the leading cause of death."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complications of CLL:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infections: Infections are a leading cause of morbidity and mortality in CLL, accounting for ~30-50% of deaths. Hypogammaglobulinemia is common in CLL and associated with mucocutaneous infections (e.g., sinusitis, bronchitis), and also causes UTIs in women. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infections are a leading cause of morbidity and mortality in CLL, accounting for ~30-50% of deaths."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypogammaglobulinemia is common in CLL and associated with mucocutaneous infections (e.g., sinusitis, bronchitis), and also causes UTIs in women. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Secondary malignancies: The most common types of cancers seen in CLL are skin, prostate, and breast cancers."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most common types of cancers seen in CLL are skin, prostate, and breast cancers."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Autoimmune complications: These most commonly include autoimmune cytopenias, but autoimmune complications of other organs include glomerulonephritis, vasculitis, and neuropathies. (Option C ruled out) Of the autoimmune cytopenias, the most common are autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) and immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These most commonly include autoimmune cytopenias, but autoimmune complications of other organs include glomerulonephritis, vasculitis, and neuropathies. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Of the autoimmune cytopenias, the most common are autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) and immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Richter’s transformation: It refers to the transformation of CLL into an aggressive lymphoma, most commonly diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), which can rarely include Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL). (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It refers to the transformation of CLL into an aggressive lymphoma, most commonly diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), which can rarely include Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL). (Option D ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Infections are a leading cause of morbidity and mortality in CLL, accounting for ~30-50% of deaths."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypogammaglobulinemia is common in CLL and associated with mucocutaneous infections (e.g., sinusitis, bronchitis), and also causes UTIs in women. (Option A ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The most common types of cancers seen in CLL are skin, prostate, and breast cancers."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These most commonly include autoimmune cytopenias, but autoimmune complications of other organs include glomerulonephritis, vasculitis, and neuropathies. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Of the autoimmune cytopenias, the most common are autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) and immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It refers to the transformation of CLL into an aggressive lymphoma, most commonly diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), which can rarely include Hodgkin Lymphoma (HL). (Option D ruled out)"}]}]);
